<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_flow" target="global_financial_fragili::asset_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained increase in weapons and munitions deliveries intensifies combat operations and the risk of supply‑chain disruptions (notably energy, agriculture and shipping), leading to sudden repricing and higher realized and implied volatility across equities, bonds and FX markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_financial_fragili</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_flow" target="middle_east_energy_attac::escalation_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained increase in weapons and munitions flows into the Ukraine theater leads Russia to divert materiel and authorize or bolster proxy and client strikes in the Middle East (to offset battlefield losses and signal deterrence), increasing the rate of hostile incidents against regional energy targets.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">middle_east_energy_attac</data>
</edge>
